  "exploiter_output": {
    "evidence": "Ran the self-contained runner in the project container. Unpatched: `file path opened by : webroot/\\..\\..\\..\\..\\windows\\win.ini` -> `RESULT: VULNERABLE` -> POV_EXIT=0. Independent trace via Utils.normalizePath+urlDecode+removeDots confirmed backslash traversal is preserved while forward-slash \"/../..\" payloads are neutralized (collapse to \"/secret.txt\"). Applied a representative fix (adding `.replace('\\\\','/')` before removeDots at StaticHandlerImpl.java:138), reran: `file path opened by : webroot/windows/win.ini` -> `RESULT: not vulnerable` -> POV_EXIT=1, then reverted the product code (verified byte-identical to original, git shows only .security-pipeline/ untracked).",
    "expected_vulnerable_behavior": "On unpatched code the POV recompiles vertx-web, invokes the genuine StaticHandlerImpl.handle() with request normalisedPath \"/%5C..%5C..%5C..%5C..%5Cwindows%5Cwin.ini\", captures the path passed to FileSystem.exists() as \"webroot/\\..\\..\\..\\..\\windows\\win.ini\", determines that this path escapes the web root when '\\' is treated as a separator, prints \"RESULT: VULNERABLE\", and exits 0. After a correct patch (normalizing/rejecting backslash traversal, e.g. so the handler resolves \"webroot/windows/win.ini\" or null), the captured path stays within the web root, the POV prints \"RESULT: not vulnerable\" and exits 1.",
    "notes": "JDK 8, Maven build. run_pov.sh recompiles the (possibly patched) vertx-web module from source and rederives the dependency classpath each run, so it always reflects the current product code. It prefers offline maven and falls back to online if needed (local ~/.m2 was seeded by `mvn -pl vertx-web -am install -DskipTests`, already done in this worktree). The filesystem-level exploit only escapes on a Windows host (where '\\' is the separator); since the pipeline container is Linux, the POV faithfully reproduces the vulnerable code path by capturing the exact filesystem argument StaticHandlerImpl produces and evaluating it under Windows separator semantics, rather than relying on Linux to follow the traversal. Helper files in the same dir: Explore.java (payload trace, not used by the runner), cp.txt/compile.log (generated build artifacts).",
    "pov_command": "bash .security-pipeline/pov/run_pov.sh",
    "pov_path": ".security-pipeline/pov/PovStaticHandlerTraversal.java",
    "regression_hint": "The POV distinguishes vulnerable vs fixed by the file path StaticHandlerImpl resolves for the encoded-backslash request: vulnerable code yields a path containing \"..\" components that escape the web root when '\\' is a separator (exit 0); a correct fix neutralizes or rejects backslash traversal so the resolved path stays under the web root or handling stops before the filesystem call (exit 1). A valid fix must strip/normalize '\\' path separators (or reject them) in the StaticHandler normalization path, not merely on the forward-slash form.",
    "status": "pov_created",
    "summary": "Created an end-to-end proof-of-vulnerability for CWE-022 path traversal in io.vertx.ext.web.handler.impl.StaticHandlerImpl (vertx-web 3.5.3.CR1). A request URL that percent-encodes Windows path separators (%5C == '\\') survives the handler's normalization chain `Utils.removeDots(Utils.urlDecode(context.normalisedPath(), false))`, because removeDots only collapses forward-slash \"../\" sequences. The POV drives the real StaticHandlerImpl.handle() with the malicious path and intercepts the exact file string the handler hands to FileSystem.exists(): `webroot/\\..\\..\\..\\..\\windows\\win.ini`. Interpreting that string the way a Windows filesystem does (backslash is a separator) shows it escapes the configured web root, disclosing arbitrary files."
